<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saifuddin Azizi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Seypidin Azizi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saifuddin Azizi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> politician from Artush who served as a minister in the Second East Turkestan Republic and in the coalition government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> from 1946. In 1949 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and supported the incorporation of Xinjiang into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. He was the first chairman of the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region from 1955 to 1968 and later held high national posts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saifuddin-azizi.jpg" alt="Saifuddin Azizi" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seypidin_Azizi_university_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saifuddin Azizi was born on 12 March 1915 into an influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> trading family originally from Artush. In 1935 he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> and studied at the Central Asia Political Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> as a Soviet agent, he helped instigate a Soviet-backed rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> in 1937, and the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a> exiled him to Tacheng as a radical young man. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> he called for resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and was for a time imprisoned by the Kuomintang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1944 he joined the Ili Rebellion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> rule in the districts of Ili, Tacheng and Altay. In the Second East Turkestan Republic he was education minister from 1945 to 1946 and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> contingent of the East Turkestan National Army. A leader of the East Turkestan Revolutionary Party, he was a delegate to the peace talks that formed the Coalition Government of Xinjiang Province in 1946, in which he directed the education <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p><p>He attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-peoples-political-consultative-conference">Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference</a> in September 1949, applied to join the CCP on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s recommendation and was admitted on 27 December, becoming vice chairman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>'s provincial government. He went with Mao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> for the Sino-Soviet Treaty of Friendship, and in December 1950 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch">Wang Zhen</a> to choose sites for the military reclamation units that grew into the Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1955 he was made a Lieutenant General of the PLA. He objected to Mao against naming the region the Xinjiang Autonomous Region, arguing that autonomy is given not to mountains and rivers but to particular nationalities, and the Central Committee adopted the name Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region. He promoted land reform and schools, and founded a flight school that trained Xinjiang's first ethnic-minority pilots.</p><p>From February 1978 he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> as a vice chairman of the Standing Committee of the fifth, sixth and seventh National People's Congresses. He died in Beijing on 24 November 2003 and was buried at the Ürümqi Revolutionary Martyrs Cemetery according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> custom. In 2017 Radio Free Asia reported that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> authorities had told bookstores to remove his books, among them his memoir The Epics of Life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seypidin Azizi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The remaining leadership, with <b>Saifuddin Azizi</b>, accepted the People's Liberation Army, and the region became part of the People's Republic of China.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-east-turkestan-republic">Second East Turkestan Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tacheng, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.75,82.98&amp;year=1972" aria-label="Tacheng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6500 521 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saifuddin Azizi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6950 791h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6950 791h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.75,82.98&amp;year=1972">What was here in 1972? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
